===Etymology 1===
[[File:Noodle Kugel.jpg|thumb|A [[serving#Noun|serving]] of kugel ''(etymology 1, {{senseno|en|dish}})''.]]

Borrowed from {{bor|en|yi|קוגל}} (referring to the inflated appearance of some puddings), from {{der|en|gmh|kugel|t=ball; globe}} (modern {{cog|de|Kugel|t=ball; (''astronomy'') celestial body, orb; (''geometry'') sphere}}); further etymology uncertain.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=9410148321|date=March 2024|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|dish}} A [[traditional#Adjective|traditional]] [[savory#Adjective|savoury]] or [[sweet#Adjective|sweet]] [[Jewish#Adjective|Jewish]] [[dish#Noun|dish]] [[consist]]ing of a [[baked#Adjective|baked]] [[pudding]] of [[pasta]], [[potato#Noun|potatoes]], or [[rice#Noun|rice]], with [[vegetable#Noun|vegetables]], or [[raisin#Noun|raisins]] and [[spice#Noun|spices]].
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Evening and Morning Meals: Menu Plan|title=The Taste of Shabbos: The Complete Sabbath Cookbook|edition=2nd|location=Jerusalem, Israel|publisher=[[w:Aish HaTorah|Aish HaTorah Women’s Organization]]|year=1988|page=56|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FU95wXi_L78C&pg=PA56|isbn=978-0-87306-426-2|passage=Many cooks prepare one '''kugel''' in honor of the Sabbath. Some families serve extra '''kugels''' in honor of a ''Yom Tov'' or ''Rosh Chodesh'' that falls on a Sabbath. Their idea is to serve as many '''kugels''' as there are Torah scrolls read in the synagogue. '''Kugel''' recipes run the gamut from sweet to salty, soft to crisp, bland to sharp. They may be baked in the oven or pan-fried on the range.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Woys Weaver|chapter=Medieval Recipes in the Polish Style|mainauthor=w:Maria Dembińska|editor=William Woys Weaver|translator=Magdalena Thomas|title=Food and Drink in Medieval Poland: Rediscovering a Cuisine of the Past|edition=revised|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=w:University of Pennsylvania Press|year=1999|page=184|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Iru2rMQR8eYC&pg=PA184|isbn=978-0-8122-3224-0|passage=In medieval Poland, '''kugel''' covered a range of preparations baked in shallow or deep (usually round) pans without reference to type of ingredients or elaborateness of preparation. The fancier deep-dish versions of '''kugel''' resembled bread puddings and were made almost exclusively for Chanukah. However, '''kugel''' also provided the practical advantage of being made in advance and then kept warm overnight for serving on Shabbos, when cooking was forbidden. '''Kugel''' made with shredded turnips is one of those old Sabbath dishes.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=[[w:Mike D|Michael Louis Diamond]], [[w:Ad-Rock|Adam Horovitz]]; {{w|Adam Yauch}} [''et al.'']|artist=the {{w|Beastie Boys}}|title=Hey Fuck You|album=w:To the 5 Boroughs|location=Hollywood, Los Angeles, Calif.|publisher=w:Capitol Records|date=14 June 2004|oclc=55658564|passage=With the dipsy-doodle, the kit and caboodle / The truth is brutal, your grandma's '''kugel'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Elinor Lipman|chapter=Julia’s Child|title=I Can‘t Complain: (All Too) Personal Essays|location=Boston, Mass.&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|year=2013|pages=5–6|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/icantcomplainall0000lipm/page/5/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-547-57620-6|passage=Our mother wanted us and loved us dearly. Her chicken and fish, her stews, her meatloaves, her lasagna and '''kugels''' and everything else were flavorful in their own, unadulterated way.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|South Africa|derogatory|slang}} A [[wealthy]] [[young#Adjective|young]] Jewish [[woman#Noun|woman]] [[characterize]]d as [[materialistic]] and [[selfish]].
#: {{synonyms|en|JAP<q:Australia, US>}}
#: {{coordinate terms|en|bagel<q:South Africa, derogatory, slang>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Matshikiza|chapter=With the Lid Off: February 1999 – May 2000|mainauthor=w:Todd Matshikiza; John Matshikiza|title=With the Lid Off: South African Insights from Home and Abroad, 1959–2000|location=Milpark, Johannesburg, Gauteng, South Africa|publisher=M&G Books|year=2000|section=part II (John Matshikiza)|page=145|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mL_QD3AQ5DUC&pg=PA145|isbn=978-0-620-26244-6|passage=She was minding her own business, taking a visitor from struggle days in London around the sights of the New South Africa, when she was recognised by a clutch of ageing '''kugels''', out doing what '''kugels''' of all ages do on a Saturday morning in Hyde Park, namely shopping.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Donnarae MacCann; [[w:Yulisa Pat Amadu Maddy|Yulisa Amadu Maddy]]|chapter=Civil Disobedience and Urban Conflict: The Apartheid Perspective|title=Apartheid and Racism in South Africa Children’s Literature: 1985–1995|series=Children’s Literature and Culture|seriesvolume=15|location=New York, N.Y.&#59; London|publisher=w:Routledge|year=2001|section=part 2 (Novels about Contemporary South Africa)|page=35|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LRLaAAAAQBAJ&pg=PA35|isbn=978-0-415-93638-5|passage=Yet [Barbara] Ludman wants to take Michelle [in ''The Day of the Kugel'' (1989)] out of the "'''Kugel'''" category (a '''Kugel''' being a "disgusting, dull, boring noodle pudding"—the people in Johannesburg suburbs whose wealthy lives are one long party).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Pieter-Dirk Uys|chapter=Bring on the Blondes|editor=Robert Plummer|title=Between the Devil and the Deep: A Memoir of Acting and Reacting|location=Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa|publisher=Zebra Press, [[w:Penguin Random House|Struik Publishers]]|year=2005|page=169|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/betweendevildeep0000uysp/page/169/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-77007-100-1|passage=The trouble is that now, in this new century, the '''kugel''' is a rare and unprotected species in South Africa. {{...}} The '''kugels''' have all left, and the few who stayed behind, protected by their high walls and heavy security, don't venture out at night to come and see themselves in a local revue. '''Kugels''' have now re-emerged in the form of black divas&nbsp;– beautiful, glamorous, loud and opinionated women who set store by hair and nails, sunglasses and cars.}}

